## E15 Tiles for the registers — QUEUED · Surface · no strategy, no Codex round

Authority: OSD-D16 (register outlines up to 8 MB until a tiling lane); E05 as merged (the page
prefers `data/outlines.pmtiles` when `files.txt` lists it); `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` (PMTiles).

**Outcome.** Opening the map on a phone connection no longer downloads the register outlines whole:
the page loads tiles for the viewport, and every feature still carries the same properties and
`feature_id` as `outlines.geojson`.

**Context today (verified 23 Oct, main after PR #7).** `outlines/sodir__factpages.geojson` is
4.3 MB and `outlines/nlog__nlog.geojson` 2.6 MB; `make build` concatenates all outlines into
`build/catalog/outlines.geojson`, which the page fetches whole. E05 added the `pmtiles` protocol
and the `files.txt` probe but nothing writes a PMTiles file. `tippecanoe` is not installed in CI.

**The design the lane must map to.**
1. `make build` writes `build/catalog/outlines.pmtiles` from `outlines.geojson` with `tippecanoe`
   (pinned version, installed in the `check` workflow from the official release or a pinned apt
   package; the build skips tiling with a visible notice when the binary is absent, so `make check`
   stays offline-capable locally). Zoom range 0 to 12, point features kept at every zoom with
   clustering left to the page, polygons simplified by tippecanoe only below zoom 8.
2. The page uses the tiles for the extents, surveys and wells layers when present and falls back to
   the GeoJSON otherwise; the list, search and detail panel keep reading `records.json` and
   `outlines.geojson` metadata (ids and names), not the tiles.
3. `feature_id` is the tile feature id; a Playwright test opens `#q=sodir`, clicks a wellbore and
   gets the same detail title as the GeoJSON path.

**Acceptance.**
- With tiles present, the page's network log on load shows no request for `outlines.geojson`
  larger than the metadata fetch (Playwright, offline, asserts the transferred bytes under 1 MB
  before any pan).
- The 10,000-wellbore performance test from E05 passes unchanged on the tile path.
- Tile feature count per layer equals the GeoJSON feature count at the maximum zoom (test using
  the `pmtiles` CLI or a Python reader).
- `make check` without `tippecanoe` prints the notice and passes.

**Out of scope.** Changes to outline files or schemas; STAC (E04).

**Ownership and dependencies.** Coding lane; paths `Makefile` (build target), `src/osd/build.py`
(tiling call only), `.github/workflows/ci.yml` and `pages.yml` (tippecanoe install step, announced),
`site/app.js`, `tests/site/**`, `tests/test_build.py` (tile count test). Depends on E03 (merged).
Runs in parallel with E04 (disjoint paths except `src/osd/build.py`: E15 adds one call at the end of
`build()`; E04 adds `stac.py` and one call; the second to merge rebases).

**Evidence to inspect.** The network-log assertion; the count test; a phone screenshot.
## E18 Items carry their own dates — QUEUED · Kernel · strategy at lane open · Codex round done 23 Oct (REWORK, dispositions below; no second round) · follow-on of E03 and E04

Authority: OSD-D14 (Items need a period), OSD-D11 (record versus tool boundary), OSD-D12 and
OSD-D16 (derived outlines are committed and name their inputs); E16 charter line assigning
per-feature dates to the outline side; E16 finding in `docs/program/HANDOFF.md` (register Items carry
the collection's period).

**Outcome.** A STAC client filtering the catalogue by time gets the wellbores drilled and the
surveys shot in that window, not every feature of a register: each Item of a register collection
carries the feature's own start and end as the publisher's service states them, normalised to
RFC 3339, with the raw value kept and the provenance of each bound marked; an Item falls back to
the collection's period only when the feature states nothing usable, and every Item interval lies
inside its Collection's extent.

**Context today (verified 23 Oct, main after PR #16).** `src/osd/stac.py` passes the record's
period to every Item (`_item`, both timestamps). The outline extractor emits identifiers, name,
kind and geometry only (`src/osd/outlines/extractors.py`); `rules.yaml` `propertyName` lists select
exactly those fields, so no date reaches an outline today. Outlines are committed files regenerated
by `osd verify --outlines` (`--outline-inputs` derives without writing); the build reads the
committed files. `schema/stac-osd.json` forbids unknown `osd:` members; `schema/outline.schema.json`
closes `inputs`. The gate validates a sample of Items (OSD-D18), `make check-full` the whole tree.
External prerequisites, to verify at lane open against the live services, not facts: Sodir
wellbore and survey date fields (E16 evidence names `Entry_year` and `Entered_date`; completion and
survey start/end field names unknown), NLOG `START_DATE_DRILLING` (five `1800-01-01` values that
E16 called "likely a placeholder") and an end-date field if one exists.

**The design the lane must map to.**
1. Rules select dates: for each register source `rules.yaml` gains `period_fields: {start, end}`
   naming service properties, and those names are added to that source's `propertyName` request
   list; an injected-transport test asserts the request carries them. `period_placeholders` per
   source lists values treated as absent (NLOG `1800-01-01` only if the lane confirms it on the
   live service; otherwise none).
2. Outlines carry them: the extractor writes `osd:period_start_raw` and `osd:period_end_raw`
   (the service's strings, verbatim) and the normalised `osd:period_start` / `osd:period_end`
   (RFC 3339 UTC instants: a date becomes `T00:00:00Z` for a start and `T23:59:59Z` for an end; a
   timestamp without zone is read as UTC; anything else is invalid and the normalised member is
   omitted). outline/3 (`$id` bumped; outline/2 files still validate under their own schema in the
   compatibility test) admits these four members and names the date fields in `inputs`. The two
   register outline files are regenerated with `--outlines`, their diffs reviewed and committed
   (OSD-D12).
3. Items: both bounds from the feature when both normalise and start ≤ end
   (`osd:period_source: feature`); feature start with the collection's end when the end is absent
   and start ≤ collection end (`osd:period_source: feature-start,collection-end`); otherwise the
   collection's period (`osd:period_source: collection`). `schema/stac-osd.json` gains the member
   with that closed enum (`$id` bumped, old files still validate).
4. Containment guard: an offline test asserts every Item interval lies within its Collection's
   extent for the fixture and, in `make check-full`, for the whole tree. If a real register fails
   it, that register keeps the collection period in this lane and the finding goes to a records
   lane (the Collection period is E16's, records-owned); the PR says which.
5. Collection extents stay the record's `period`; the PR reports per register: Items with own
   dates, mixed, fallback; min and max feature dates against the collection period; named Item
   examples before and after.

**Acceptance.**
- Injected-transport test: the WFS request for each register includes the date fields; removing
  them from `propertyName` fails it.
- Fixture features (distinct fixture collection period, 1990–2000): dated → own bounds, exact
  RFC 3339 strings asserted per named feature id; undated → collection bounds and `collection`;
  placeholder → treated as undated; reversed → collection and `collection`; open end → feature
  start plus collection end and the mixed provenance; start after the collection end with no end
  → collection; malformed string → raw kept, normalised omitted, collection used; timestamp without
  zone → read as UTC.
- Containment test green on the fixture; `make check-full` containment on the tree.
- Compatibility: outline/2 fixture files and STAC files without `osd:period_source` validate under
  their own schemas; the schema map carries both versions.
- Mutations against the unchanged suite: placeholder rule dropped; fallback dropped; reversed
  accepted; dates read from the record instead of the feature; raw not kept; mixed provenance
  mislabelled `feature`; containment guard dropped → each red.
- After merge: the published Sodir and NLOG trees have the named example Items with the asserted
  bounds (post-publication check in HANDOFF).

**Out of scope.** Collection periods (E16, records); Items for records without outlines; records.

**Stop rules.** A register whose service exposes no per-feature dates keeps the collection period;
the lane says so and does not scrape pages.

**Ownership and dependencies.** Coding lane; paths `src/osd/outlines/rules.yaml`,
`src/osd/outlines/extractors.py`, `src/osd/outlines/*.py`, `src/osd/stac.py`, `src/osd/verify.py`
(only if the outline writer needs the date fields passed), `schema/outline.schema.json` (outline/3),
`schema/stac-osd.json` (`osd:period_source`; shared with E19's access enum: second to merge
rebases), `schema/stac/schema-map.json`, `records/outlines/sodir--factpages.geojson` and
`records/outlines/nlog--nlog.geojson` (or wherever the committed register outlines live; name at
lane open), `tests/test_outlines.py`, `tests/test_stac.py`, `tests/fixtures/outlines/**`,
`docs/program/evidence/e18/**`. Depends on E03, E04, E16 (merged).

**Evidence to inspect.** The per-register counts; two named Items before and after; the
containment result; the mutation list; the outline diff summary.

**Dispositions of the Codex round (23 Oct).** Accepted: context corrected (call path, extractor
emits fixed fields, propertyName lists, field names and the 1800 placeholder are prerequisites not
facts, no generated tree in the checkout); schemas claimed and versioned (outline/3, stac-osd
`$id`), schema map and compatibility tests; the committed register outlines claimed and regenerated
with `--outlines`; date fields added to the requests with an injected-transport test; normalisation
and invalid handling defined, raw kept; open-end and mixed provenance defined with a validity rule;
containment guard added with deferral to a records lane; acceptance rewritten with exact instants
on named ids, distinct fixture period, post-publication check, and mutations for each guard; E18
named a follow-on of E03/E04. Refuted: none.
## E29 Outlines from the registers' map services — QUEUED · Kernel · strategy at lane open · Codex round done 23 Oct (REWORK, dispositions below; no second round)

Authority: owner direction 23 Oct 2026 (levels on the map are real; L3 means the outlines were
read from the data); E03 (outline extractor, record `inputs`, outline/2 and /3, the WFS format,
the stored hit count rule), E16 design 2 (a register's own coverage period, as the publisher
states it), E18 (`period_fields`, date normalisation and provenance, Items carry their own dates),
E15 (tiles), E23 (the ArcGIS listing reader; exports stop at the layer's `maxRecordCount`, kept as
`partial_exports`); OSD-D18; AGENTS.md schema-change rule; `docs/ENGINEERING.md`.

**Outcome.** Registers that publish an ArcGIS map service show their wells and surveys on the map
as points and outlines read from the service itself; their records reach L3; and where the record
carries the register's publisher-stated period, its STAC Collection holds one Item per feature
with the dates the service states.

**Context today (verified 23 Oct, main 7d86017; Codex-checked).** Five records carry outline
`inputs` (`records/sodir/factpages.yaml:25`, `nlog/nlog.yaml:22`, `nopims/poseidon.yaml:23`,
`nzpam/kerry-3d.yaml:21`, `nzpam/parihaka.yaml:22`). `src/osd/outlines/` reads `format: wfs`
(GeoJSON pages, `__init__.py:115-132`); the WFS count check (`extractors.py:150-155`) runs only
when a hits part with a parseable `numberMatched` exists; field rules live in `rules.yaml:28-83`;
`osd verify --outline-inputs` compares committed outlines with today's inputs
(`verify.yml:40`) and never writes outlines (`--outlines` does); a refused input fails
`outline-derived` (`__init__.py:250-251`). `schema/record.schema.json:104` limits
`inputs[].format` to today's values, so `arcgis` needs record/5 (record/4 landed with E26,
PR #34). `src/osd/stac.py:163-165` emits no Items for a record without `period`. Thirteen records
list an ArcGIS layer (`samples.yaml:19,20,43,50,51,56,58,61,63,64,76,77,78`), counts as observed on
23 Oct: nzpam/petroleum-wells 1,302; nzpam/seismic-surveys-2d 990; wa-demirs/petroleum-wells
4,371; sgudds/register-geotermalnych-vrtov 307; va-energy/gas-oil-wells 8,605; nd-dmr/wells
44,018; bcer/well-surface-holes-permitted, sgc/banco-informacion-petrolera-pozos and
ut-dogm/oil-gas-wells (totals not recorded); sk-mer/vertical-wells 119,595; sk-mer/drill-core
104,754; pgi/cbdg-boreholes 334,145; cgs/vrtna-prozkoumanost 746,789. Of the nine candidates,
six have no `period` (both NZP&M, SGUDDS, Virginia, North Dakota, BCER). Verify on main after
E24 (run 39981701762) took 40 minutes under the 60-minute job timeout (`verify.yml:15`).
Live counts, pagination support and run time are observations to re-establish on the day.

**The design the lane must map to.**
1. record/5: `inputs[].format` gains `arcgis`; record/4 frozen under `tests/fixtures/schema/`, one
   fixed fixture set validates under both, every stored record validates under record/5; the PR
   title names the schema change.
2. `format: arcgis` in `src/osd/outlines/`: read the layer description (`?f=json`); refuse the
   input unless `supportsPagination` is true and an `objectIdField` is named; page with
   `orderByFields=<objectIdField> ASC`, `resultOffset`, `resultRecordCount=maxRecordCount`,
   `outFields=*`, native spatial reference (no `outSR`); the response's `spatialReference.wkid`
   must match the record's declared `crs` else refuse; `f=geojson` when `supportedQueryFormats`
   lists it, else `f=json` converted (point, multipoint, polyline with several paths, polygon
   with holes and several exteriors per Esri ring orientation); a feature with null geometry is
   listed in `unplaced`. Identity: object ids must be unique across pages and their number must
   equal `returnCountOnly=true` on the day; a duplicate, a missing page, a page error or a count
   mismatch refuses the input. Coordinates are transformed from the declared `crs` exactly as the
   WFS path does today. Levels are recomputed from today's checks: a refused input fails
   `outline-derived`, and a record that was L3 is demoted if its input is refused today.
3. Dates: the fields `rules.yaml` names per input (E18 `period_fields`); epoch milliseconds to
   RFC 3339 UTC, the raw value kept in `osd:period_*_raw`; E18's provenance rules unchanged
   (`feature` when the feature states dates, else the existing fallbacks; `stac.py:109-118`).
4. Budget: an input is read in full or refused; the four layers above 50,000 features (SK MER x2,
   PGI, CGS) are not given inputs this lane. The duration proof is one all-record dispatch of
   `verify.yml` on the branch; it must finish under 60 minutes and `make check-full` must pass on
   its outputs. The lane adds inputs in order of size (SGUDDS, NZP&M x2, WA DEMIRS, Virginia,
   BCER, SGC, UT DOGM, North Dakota) and stops adding when the dispatch passes 50 minutes; the
   remaining ones are deferred with the measured time, no infrastructure proposal.
5. Records, exact edits, these nine and no others: `nzpam/petroleum-wells`,
   `nzpam/seismic-surveys-2d`, `wa-demirs/petroleum-wells`, `sgudds/register-geotermalnych-vrtov`,
   `va-energy/gas-oil-wells`, `nd-dmr/wells`, `bcer/well-surface-holes-permitted`,
   `sgc/banco-informacion-petrolera-pozos`, `ut-dogm/oil-gas-wells`: `inputs` (name, the layer
   URL without a query, `format: arcgis`, `crs` and `stated` from the service description), and
   `period` for the six without one where the register's page or service description states its
   coverage (E16 design 2; the publisher's sentence in `notes`); a register that states none keeps
   no period and no Items, said in the PR. Outlines for these records are generated with
   `--outlines`, reviewed, committed under `outlines/`, then verified with `--outline-inputs`.
6. Site: nothing new; tiles (E15) carry the points. One phone-width screenshot of a new register
   is evidence.

**Acceptance.**
- Fixture ArcGIS server (injected responses, offline), per input: pages reassemble the layer with
  the exact object-id set; duplicates, a missing page, a page error, a count mismatch, missing
  pagination support, a wkid mismatch each refuse; `f=json` conversion of a point, a two-path
  polyline, a polygon with a hole and a two-exterior polygon matches GeoJSON fixtures; epoch-ms
  dates match given RFC 3339 strings with distinct feature and collection dates so a fallback
  cannot hide a broken conversion. Mutations, one per guard: count check removed; duplicate check
  removed; page error swallowed; cap ignored; wkid check removed; hole dropped; date field
  dropped; off-by-a-day conversion.
- STAC fixture: for one fixture record with a period, the Item id set equals the object-id set and
  named Items carry exact dates and `osd:period_source: feature`; suppressing the Items fails.
- Live: at least four of the nine records at L3 on the branch dispatch with their feature counts
  stated (the expected count is the day's `returnCountOnly`), or per-record outage evidence
  (HTTP status and time) for those that are not; universal refusal is a failed lane.
- The all-record branch dispatch finishes under 60 minutes, `make check-full` passes on its
  outputs; both stated in the PR with the run id.
- Record diffs show only `inputs` and, where added, `period` and the `notes` sentence.

**Out of scope.** Layers above 50,000 features; WFS or other formats; the verifier's ArcGIS
listing reader; Collection extents; any site code; any infrastructure change (ENGINEERING.md:28).

**Stop rules.** If no new input fits under the 60-minute dispatch, the lane ships the fixture
work, the schema and the record `inputs` behind refusal (no outlines), records the measured
times per input, and defers the rest; no charter for infrastructure is proposed by this lane.

**Ownership and dependencies.** Coding lane; paths `schema/record.schema.json` (record/5),
`tests/fixtures/schema/record-4.schema.json` (new), `tests/test_schema.py` (compatibility
additions), `tests/test_outlines.py` (its `$id` lines and additions), `src/osd/outlines/**`,
`src/osd/verify.py` (outline input dispatch only, if needed), `tests/fixtures/outlines/**`,
`outlines/**` for the nine records, the nine records in design 5, `docs/program/evidence/e29/**`.
Depends on E03, E18, E23, E25, E26 (merged). E28 (periods) holds no record of these nine.

**Evidence to inspect.** The fixture tests and the eight mutations; the dispatch run id, its
duration and `check-full`; the record diffs; one screenshot.

**Dispositions of the Codex round (23 Oct).** Accepted: thirteen ArcGIS records, nine candidates
named exactly; record/5 for `inputs[].format` with frozen record/4 and compatibility tests; the
period prerequisite resolved inside the lane (E16 design 2 register periods for the six records,
publisher-stated, else no Items and said so); identity contract (objectIdField, ordered paging,
duplicates and count both checked, refusal when pagination is unsupported); native CRS with a
wkid check and the existing transform; geometry and date conversion specified with provenance per
E18; "keeps its level" replaced by recomputation and demotion; acceptance requires named live
successes with counts, exact Item id sets and dates in fixtures, an all-record branch dispatch
with `make check-full`, eight mutations, and the `--outlines` derivation step; the truncation
sentence corrected to `maxRecordCount`; the stop rule records limits and defers, proposing no
infrastructure. Refuted: none.
